Treat your furry companion to wholesome, oven-baked dog biscuits made with pantry staples you can pronounce. Whole wheat flour provides hearty fiber while natural peanut butter and a touch of honey deliver irresistible flavor dogs love. Finished with a pet-safe, sugar-free cornstarch and peanut butter drizzle.

*Adapted from [The Cookie Rookie](https://www.thecookierookie.com/pb-dog-treats/).*

## Ingredients

### Peanut Butter Biscuit Dough
- 2 ½ cups whole wheat flour (approx. 300g)
- 1 large egg
- 1 cup natural creamy peanut butter (check the label: **MUST BE 100% XYLITOL-FREE**)
- 1 cup water
- 2 tablespoons pure honey

### Pet-Safe Icing
- 2 tablespoons creamy peanut butter (xylitol-free)
- 1 tablespoon pure honey
- ⅓ cup cornstarch (or arrowroot or potato starch)
- 3 to 4 tablespoons warm water

## Instructions

1. **Preheat Oven and Prep Pan:** Position an oven rack in the center and preheat the oven to 350°F (175°C). Line a large rimmed baking sheet with parchment paper.
2. **Safety Check:** Always double-check the ingredient label on your peanut butter. Never use peanut butter sweetened with xylitol (also labeled as birch bark extract or birch sugar), which is highly toxic to dogs.
3. **Mix the Dough:** In a large bowl (or stand mixer fitted with the paddle attachment), combine the whole wheat flour and egg. Pour in 1 cup of natural peanut butter, the water, and 2 tablespoons of honey. Mix thoroughly. The dough will become very dense, stiff, and slightly tacky. Use your hands to knead it in the bowl for 1 minute into a cohesive ball.
4. **Roll and Cut:** Lightly dust a clean countertop with whole wheat flour. Roll the dough out with a floured rolling pin to an even ½-inch thickness. Press out shapes using dog bone, fire hydrant, or paw print cookie cutters. Gather dough scraps, re-roll, and cut until all dough is used.
5. **Bake to Crisp:** Place cutouts onto the prepared baking sheet, leaving ½ inch of space between them (these treats do not spread or rise). Bake for 18 to 20 minutes. For an extra-crunchy biscuit that helps clean canine teeth, turn off the oven and leave the treats inside for an extra 10 to 15 minutes with the door propped slightly open to dehydrate. Transfer to a wire rack to cool completely.
6. **Make the Pet-Safe Icing:** In a small microwave-safe dish, warm 2 tablespoons of peanut butter and 1 tablespoon of honey for 10 to 15 seconds until melted and liquid. Whisk this into ⅓ cup of cornstarch. Gradually drizzle in warm water 1 tablespoon at a time, whisking briskly until you achieve a smooth frosting with the consistency of royal icing.
7. **Decorate and Set:** Transfer the frosting into a small piping bag or zip-top bag with the corner snipped. Pipe paw prints, names, or zigzag stripes across the cooled biscuits. Let the decorated biscuits sit at room temperature for 1 hour so the cornstarch icing firms to the touch.

## Notes

- **Flour Alternatives:** If your dog has a wheat sensitivity, substitute oat flour (made by finely grinding rolled oats in a blender) or brown rice flour.
- **Storage:** Store un-iced or fully dried iced biscuits in an airtight jar or container at room temperature for up to 2 weeks, or freeze in a freezer bag for up to 6 months.
